00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.353
Hey everyone, what is DNS?

00:00:03.353 --> 00:00:07.040
Have you ever wondered how you &nbsp;
access the web pages that you do

00:00:07.200 --> 00:00:08.628
almost every other day?

00:00:08.628 --> 00:00:10.960
I am Pooja, from IBM team

00:00:10.960 --> 00:00:14.057
and I'm going to explain to you how DNS works.

00:00:14.135 --> 00:00:18.346
DNS in short is called the Domain Name System.

00:00:18.346 --> 00:00:22.320
The domain name system translates domain names

00:00:22.320 --> 00:00:29.099
to IP addresses, and what is a&nbsp;
domain and what is an IP address?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.357 --> 00:00:31.295
Let's take a look at that.

00:00:31.737 --> 00:00:34.282
First,&nbsp;let's see what a domain name is.

00:00:35.360 --> 00:00:41.081
A domain name is any text or string that you&nbsp;
basically enter in your web page. &nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.153 --> 00:00:46.917
It could be ibm.com, google.com, abc.com, any of these.

00:00:47.045 --> 00:00:49.744
And what really is an IP address?

00:00:50.016 --> 00:00:54.022
IP address is a four set number, goes like this.

00:00:54.022 --> 00:00:57.840
Just&nbsp;going to write a random number for you here.

00:00:59.280 --> 00:01:03.759
I'm pretty sure you have all&nbsp;seen 
this IP address almost&nbsp;every day 

00:01:03.759 --> 00:01:07.815
when you access these websites and says&nbsp;
oh you know your web browser is down. &nbsp;

00:01:07.815 --> 00:01:10.821
You know, you're not able to access 
the DNS that you're looking for.

00:01:10.821 --> 00:01:14.848
So, let's take an example, abc.com.

00:01:15.600 --> 00:01:21.680
What happens is, instead of typing abc.com, if you&nbsp;
type in the IP address of that particular website&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.680 --> 00:01:26.106
in your web browser you will actually &nbsp;
be routed to the web page.

00:01:26.106 --> 00:01:30.400
Now, the main component of the DNS system

00:01:30.400 --> 00:01:32.294
is the DNS resolver.

00:01:32.665 --> 00:01:38.544
So, the DNS resolver&nbsp;acts as the 
phone book in this entire thing.

00:01:39.440 --> 00:01:43.088
How do we bridge the gap between &nbsp;
the human communication

00:01:43.088 --> 00:01:45.949
and the DNS, and the networking world?

00:01:45.949 --> 00:01:51.760
So, in the networking world the computers&nbsp;
use numbers to communicate with each other, and in

00:01:51.760 --> 00:01:58.240
the human world we use names to communicate with&nbsp;
each other, and here DNS resolver acts as a phone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.240 --> 00:02:05.440
book where you search the name and match it to the&nbsp;
number. The IP address is the internet protocol&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.440 --> 00:02:10.880
that has a set of rules that helps millions of&nbsp;
devices communicate with each other and that's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.880 --> 00:02:15.360
why we have these IP addresses allocated&nbsp;
to each and every device on the internet.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.480 --> 00:02:22.720
All these devices communicate using this&nbsp;
unique identifier, and since as humans&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.720 --> 00:02:29.120
we are not accustomed to remembering names, we&nbsp;
use these domain names, to remembering numbers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.120 --> 00:02:35.120
we use the domain names and that's how we&nbsp;
resolve using the DNS resolver. Now that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.120 --> 00:02:41.280
we know an overview of the domain name system&nbsp;
let's take a look at how the DNS lookup happens.

00:02:43.680 --> 00:02:46.880
So the first step in the DNS lookup is the user.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.840 --> 00:02:55.360
This is where we enter the website address from&nbsp;
our web page and you are going to be the actor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.400 --> 00:03:06.080
here. So, the web browser has what we call the&nbsp;
cache memory. The cache memory stores certain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.080 --> 00:03:15.120
values for a certain time period of time. So, when&nbsp;
you enter the address abc.com let's say, what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.120 --> 00:03:21.760
happens is it looks in the cache memory first and&nbsp;
when we say, "oh, i don't have the IP address of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.760 --> 00:03:27.760
the particular website that you're looking for,"&nbsp;
we forward the request to the main DNS resolver.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.680 --> 00:03:32.400
So this is going to be our&nbsp;
DNS resolver for the day,

00:03:37.040 --> 00:03:41.600
and now from here the DNS&nbsp;
resolver has its own cache.

00:03:43.760 --> 00:03:50.000
It looks at its cache and says "hey, i don't have&nbsp;
the IP address of the particular website that you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.000 --> 00:03:55.280
are looking for. So what i'm going to do is i'm&nbsp;
going to route your request to the root server."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.960 --> 00:04:03.040
I'm going to use a different&nbsp;
color. So, this is the root server&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.400 --> 00:04:10.160
and root server is the top server at&nbsp;
the top level in the DNS hierarchy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.440 --> 00:04:16.560
and the root server still doesn't have the&nbsp;
information of abc.com that you're looking for,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.120 --> 00:04:22.000
but what it does have is the information&nbsp;
about the top level domain server&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.000 --> 00:04:28.720
that you have to route your request to. Also the&nbsp;
root server is placed across different locations&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.720 --> 00:04:33.120
throughout the world there are almost&nbsp;
hundreds of these root servers placed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.120 --> 00:04:39.040
and there are 12 different organizations that&nbsp;
manage these root servers, and from there we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.040 --> 00:04:45.440
get the top level domain server's IP address.&nbsp;
So, now we send the request to the TLD server.

00:04:47.600 --> 00:04:54.800
And the TLD is nothing but "Top Level Domain",&nbsp;
which basically means it has all the information&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.800 --> 00:04:59.040
for the top level domain, and in this&nbsp;
case the top level domain is going to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.040 --> 00:05:07.760
a dot com. So it could be anything like dot&nbsp;
net, dot org, as such. What it does say is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.320 --> 00:05:15.200
"hey, i don't have the ip address of abc.com, but&nbsp;
i can send your request to the authoritative name&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.200 --> 00:05:19.520
server. I can send the IP address to which&nbsp;
you need to send your request to, and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.520 --> 00:05:26.080
authoritative name server has all the information&nbsp;
and all the DNS records that we need to access.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.080 --> 00:05:31.520
So basically the authoritative name server&nbsp;
renders the DNS records that we need to access.

00:05:35.120 --> 00:05:38.240
So the request is sent to the auth name server,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.280 --> 00:05:44.320
oh and we can also call it as the domain&nbsp;
name server, and lot of cloud providers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.320 --> 00:05:51.840
across all over the world provide this server&nbsp;
ability, and you can use multitude of them.

00:05:54.720 --> 00:05:59.840
So this is going to be our authoritative name&nbsp;
server, and what the authoritative name server&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.840 --> 00:06:04.640
does it sends back the IP address of the&nbsp;
particular website that we are looking for,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.440 --> 00:06:11.760
and now we get the IP address back to the&nbsp;
DNS resolver so the DNS resolver now stores&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.760 --> 00:06:16.960
it in the cache. I store it in the cache&nbsp;
and I send it back to the web browser,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.720 --> 00:06:24.480
and now the web browser has the IP address&nbsp;
that it was looking for. What it does is it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.480 --> 00:06:30.320
sends the request to the particular web server&nbsp;
that points in the IP address that's been sent.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.960 --> 00:06:36.320
So, this is the web server of any particular&nbsp;
website that you're probably accessing. In&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.320 --> 00:06:44.240
this case it is going to be abc.com. So, now&nbsp;
the web server has all the content that is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.240 --> 00:06:50.560
needed to display in your web page. What we&nbsp;
render back is the content of your web page.

00:06:52.800 --> 00:07:00.720
This is the entire working of our DNS lookup and&nbsp;
this is what we almost do in every day when we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.720 --> 00:07:06.560
access different websites all over the internet.&nbsp;
I hope you find this video very informative.

